\begin_layout Section
User instructions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This brief manual outlines the steps required to compile and simulate a
 C program on the custom-designed RISC-V 32-bit soft-core processor.
 To successfully compile and simulate programs on this processor, ensure
 the following tools are installed and properly configured:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GNU RISC-V 32-bit Toolchain (target: riscv32-unknown-elf)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Xilinx Vivado Design Suite
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The flow of running a C code on the processor involves two main stages:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compilation of C Code to Instruction and Data Memory Files: Before simulation,
 your C source code needs to be compiled into RISC-V assembly and then converted
 to .mem files required for initializing the FPGA memory.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Compile .c to .s (Assembly): Use the following command to compile your C code:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc -S -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -o program.s program.c
 
\series default
: This will generate an assembly file program.s from your C source program.c.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Generate instruction.mem and data.mem: Run the provided script:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
./generate_testcase.sh program.s rv32i_linker.ld entry.s
\series default
: This script will generate instruction.mem and data.mem (if your program
 contains a data section).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Running Simulation on Vivado: Once you have the memory files, run the simulation
 using Vivado in batch mode:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
vivado -mode batch -source run_test.tcl 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Important Notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Ensure that your assembly code or compiled program ends with an ebreak instructi
on.
 This is essential for the processor to halt gracefully after program execution
 and for the testbench to detect completion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You can inspect simulation logs and waveform data (if enabled) to verify
 register values, memory states, and instruction flow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
To compile and run the design for ASIC synthesis, follow these instruction:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
RTL Compilation (
\emph on
Xcillium
\emph default
): xrun -f filelist.f -top riscv_platform -access+rwc 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
RTL Simulation (
\emph on
Xcillium
\emph default
): RTL simultion can be run in 2 ways, either a directed test case or a
 regression.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Direct Test: ./run_sim.tcl +test=<test_name> -gui
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Regression: ./run_test_all.tcl
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
LINTINT (
\emph on
Jasper gold
\emph default
): jg -superlint -script run_lint.tcl
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Genus synthesis: For genus synthesis we should have following files
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
RTL Files: ./RTL_Sources/*
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Library Files: ./Synthesis/lib...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
SDC Constraints: ./Constraints/core.sdc
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
genus run script: ./Scripts/core_run.tcl
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
To run the genus synthesis, follow these commands
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
genus -gui
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
source ./Scripts/core_run.tcl
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
This will generate the netlist.v files, along with the sdf annotation files
 required for the Gate level simulations.
 Report files like timing report, area report, power report, critical STA
 paths will be dumped by the tool.
 LEC do files will also be dumped 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Corrections
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are currently making the Soc from our processor which will integrate
 core with peripherals like UART, CLINT, SPI, PWM etc.
 These will be used for the DEMO purposes for the FPGA board.
 Also the debug is currently under testing, hence commands for invoking
 debug is still not mentioned.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Suggestions for next gen
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Currently the RTL is matured enough to be used as a soft-core IP.
 Also the RTL is synthesizable for ASIC flow.
 For further batches, it is strictly recommended to go ahead with the ASIC
 flow in following direction:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Understand the RTL and design thoroughly, run vivado simulations and testcases
 for better understanding 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Understand the ASIC flow, go through the documentation of CDAC and sample
 FIFO project to get a hands-on practice of the flow.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We'll be moving to the lower technology nodes (45nm or 28nm), that will
 introduce mutiple challenges like STA, hold violations, new memory models
 etc.
 So replace all the previous memory models with the newer ones, and update
 the library files in the genus scripts.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Synthesize the RTL and check whether all the testcases are passing SDF simulatio
n, there should be no 'X' propogation in design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Look for critical paths in the design and try to optimize the design for
 performance.
 Majorly the paths via Dcache needs to be optimised for increasing the clock
 frequency.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Once the synthesized RTL is verified, go for placement of macros, power-net
 connectivity, routing and layouting of the design.
 Clear all the DRC and LVS errors (if any).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run the post-route timing simulations on different PVT corners.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If everything works fine, we can go ahead with the GDS (Tape-out).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Future scope
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The core can be outsourced to different vendors for creating a Soc for low-power
 application like power meters and watches.
 Also the core can be used as a microcontroller, which can be used for various
 embedded applications.
 We can build a PCB around the processor, which can serve the need of in-house
 computings and various applications then can be build upon it.
 It will eliminate the need for relying on ARM based microcontrollers.
 
\end_layout

